A host constant is a value, not something to call. `math.Pi` is a Go constant,
`Math.PI` a JavaScript property, `StrokeCap.Round` a Kotlin property on a
companion object -- none has a call form, and writing one is a compile error on
two of the three and a runtime TypeError on the other.

So the declaration is a `const` or a `var` with no initializer and a native
mark. The form says what the flag would have had to: neither names a call, which
no `func` shape can state, since `func pi() float` is identical whether the host
thing is a function or a field.

Which of the two is the host's own distinction. A const is what the program only
reads; a `var` names a host global it may also **assign** to, which is the whole
reason the mark is not restricted to consts -- and why the identifier has to be
resolved on the write path as well as the read one. Answered only on the read
side, `hostArgs = []` came out as an assignment to a bare name this package
declares nowhere.

Nothing is emitted for the declaration -- it *is* the host identifier -- and
every reference resolves to the name the mark carries, with the import it
names. The rule a native func already follows, now shared by
`ir.IsHostValue`.

One mark, because `ir.Foreign` holds one scheme and one name: a declaration
that carried both a `go.native` and a `js.native` would keep whichever was
applied last, silently, and the Go output said `Math.PI`. That is a
pre-existing hole in the native marks -- it bites a func exactly as hard -- and
is not this fixture's subject; a value several languages each spell
differently is an `#[marks.intrinsic]`, which is how `math.sin` already does
it.

deny out/go/bubbletea/model.go `func pi\(` -- a native is never emitted; the declaration is the host's
deny out/go/bubbletea/model.go `pi\b.*=.*0` -- the value lives on the host, so there is no zero to normalize it to
deny out/go/bubbletea/model.go `math\.Pi\(\)` -- a Go constant has no call form
deny out/go/bubbletea/model.go `\bhostArgs\b` -- the write resolves to the host identifier, not to the SNGL name

-- app.sngl --
import ui "sngl:ui"
import go "sngl:language/go"

output {
    go {
        bubbletea
    }
}

#[go.native("math", "math.Pi")]
const pi float

#[go.native("os", "os.Args")]
var hostArgs list[string]

ui.window(title="t") {
    var turns = 2.0

    ui.text(value=string(pi * turns))
    ui.button(text="more", @click {
        turns = turns + 1.0
        hostArgs = []
    })
}
